Availability: SDD

Purpose:
  Define availability levels, RTO/RPO targets, HA patterns, disaster
  recovery strategies, backup rules, and failover mechanisms. Availability
  is a non-functional requirement that must be selected based on business
  criticality, not defaulted to the highest level.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Availability levels (99% to 99.999%)
  RTO (Recovery Time Objective)
  RPO (Recovery Point Objective)
  HA patterns (active-passive, active-active, multi-zone, multi-region)
  Disaster recovery
  Backup strategies
  Failover mechanisms

Principles:
  Availability MUST be selected based on business criticality.
  Higher availability requires proportional investment.
  RTO and RPO MUST be defined before architecture selection.
  Backup is NOT a substitute for high availability.
  Failover MUST be tested regularly.

AvailabilityLevels:

  L99:
    name: Standard
    downtime_year: 3.65 days
    downtime_month: 7.31 hours
    rto: 24 hours
    rpo: 24 hours
    applicable:
      - internal tools
      - non-critical systems
      - prototypes
    pattern:
      - single instance
      - daily backups
      - manual recovery

  L99_9:
    name: Production
    downtime_year: 8.76 hours
    downtime_month: 43.83 minutes
    rto: 4 hours
    rpo: 1 hour
    applicable:
      - production SaaS
      - business platforms
      - customer-facing apps
    pattern:
      - multi-instance
      - automated backups
      - automated failover

  L99_95:
    name: High Availability
    downtime_year: 4.38 hours
    downtime_month: 21.92 minutes
    rto: 1 hour
    rpo: 15 minutes
    applicable:
      - financial systems
      - payment processing
      - critical business functions
    pattern:
      - multi-zone
      - active-passive
      - near-real-time replication

  L99_99:
    name: Critical
    downtime_year: 52.56 minutes
    downtime_month: 4.38 minutes
    rto: 15 minutes
    rpo: 5 minutes
    applicable:
      - payment systems
      - authentication systems
      - regulated systems
    pattern:
      - multi-zone active-active
      - automated failover
      - real-time replication

  L99_999:
    name: Mission Critical
    downtime_year: 5.26 minutes
    downtime_month: 26.30 seconds
    rto: 1 minute
    rpo: seconds
    applicable:
      - global platforms
      - critical infrastructure
      - government systems
    pattern:
      - multi-region active-active
      - automated failover
      - real-time synchronous replication
      - chaos engineering

RTO_RPO:

  RTO:
    description: Maximum acceptable downtime
    measurement: time from failure to service restoration
    selection_factors:
      - business impact of downtime
      - revenue loss per hour
      - user experience impact
      - regulatory requirements

  RPO:
    description: Maximum acceptable data loss
    measurement: time between last backup and failure
    selection_factors:
      - data criticality
      - transaction volume
      - regulatory requirements
      - recovery feasibility

  relationship:
    RPO <= backup frequency
    RTO >= estimated recovery time
    both MUST be documented in ../projects/vkard.az/decisions/INDEX.sdd

HAPatterns:

  ActivePassive:
    description: Primary handles traffic, standby takes over on failure
    use_when:
      - simple failover
      - stateful applications
      - L99.9 and below
    failover:
      - automated: health check failure triggers promotion
      - manual: human decision required
    data_sync:
      - synchronous: no data loss, higher latency
      - asynchronous: possible data loss, lower latency

  ActiveActive:
    description: Multiple nodes handle traffic simultaneously
    use_when:
      - stateless services
      - high availability requirements
      - L99.99 and above
    requirements:
      - shared state in distributed cache or DB
      - conflict resolution for writes
      - session affinity avoidance
    benefits:
      - zero downtime failover
      - load distribution
      - geographic distribution

  MultiZone:
    description: Deploy across multiple availability zones within a region
    use_when:
      - L99.95 and above
      - single region acceptable
    requirements:
      - zone-independent design
      - cross-zone data replication
      - zone-aware load balancing
    failure_mode:
      - zone failure: traffic shifts to healthy zones
      - automatic detection and routing

  MultiRegion:
    description: Deploy across multiple geographic regions
    use_when:
      - L99.999
      - global user base
      - disaster recovery requirements
    requirements:
      - region-independent design
      - cross-region data replication
      - geographic DNS routing
    benefits:
      - disaster recovery
      - latency reduction
      - regulatory compliance

BackupStrategies:

  FullBackup:
    description: Complete copy of all data
    frequency:
      - L99: daily
      - L99.9: every 12 hours
      - L99.95: every 6 hours
      - L99.99: every hour
      - L99.999: real-time replication
    storage:
      - off-site storage
      - immutable backups
      - encrypted in transit and at rest

  IncrementalBackup:
    description: Only changed data since last backup
    frequency: hourly or more frequent
    benefit: faster backup, less storage
    requirement: full backup as baseline

  ContinuousBackup:
    description: Real-time backup via replication
    use_when: L99.99 and above
    method: write-ahead log shipping or synchronous replication

  BackupValidation:
    rule: backups MUST be tested by restoration at least quarterly
    rule: restoration time MUST be measured and compared to RTO
    rule: backup failures MUST alert immediately

FailoverMechanisms:

  AutomatedFailover:
    description: Automatic detection and promotion of standby
    when_to_use:
      - L99.9 and above
      - stateless or active-active
    requirements:
      - health check interval < 30 seconds
      - promotion time < RTO
      - data consistency verification
    anti_pattern:
      - split-brain: MUST prevent dual-primary

  ManualFailover:
    description: Human-initiated failover
    when_to_use:
      - L99 and below
      - complex stateful systems
    requirement: runbook MUST exist
    requirement: drill MUST be performed quarterly

  GracefulDegradation:
    description: Reduce functionality rather than fail completely
    examples:
      - disable non-critical features
      - serve cached responses
      - queue requests for later processing
    rule: degraded mode MUST be explicitly designed

DisasterRecovery:

  DRPlan:
    description: Documented procedure for disaster recovery
    must_include:
      - RTO and RPO targets
      - recovery procedures by failure type
      - escalation contacts
      - communication plan
      - rollback procedure

  DRTesting:
    frequency:
      - L99: annually
      - L99.9: semi-annually
      - L99.95: quarterly
      - L99.99: monthly
      - L99.999: continuous (chaos engineering)
    rule: test results MUST be documented and reviewed

  DRTypes:
    backup_restore:
      - restore from backup to new infrastructure
      - longest RTO, lowest cost
      - applicable: L99-L99.9

    pilot_light:
      - minimal infrastructure running, scale up on failure
      - medium RTO, medium cost
      - applicable: L99.95

    warm_standby:
      - scaled-down version running, scale up on failure
      - low RTO, high cost
      - applicable: L99.99

    multi_site_active:
      - full infrastructure running in multiple regions
      - lowest RTO, highest cost
      - applicable: L99.999

Selection:

  AI MUST select availability level based on:
    business criticality
    revenue impact of downtime
    data loss tolerance
    regulatory requirements
    budget
    team capability

  Availability MUST NOT be selected at maximum level by default —
  it MUST be proportional to actual business need.

AntiPatterns:

  MaximumByDefault:
    description: Selecting 99.999% without business justification
    rule: MUST have documented business case for L99.999

  BackupAsHA:
    description: Relying on backups instead of HA infrastructure
    rule: backups complement HA, they do not replace it

  UntestedFailover:
    description: Failover mechanism never tested
    rule: MUST test failover per DRTesting frequency above

  SingleRegionProduction:
    description: Production in single region without DR plan
    rule: L99.95 and above MUST have documented DR plan

  SilentDegradation:
    description: Degradation without alerting
    rule: degraded mode MUST generate alerts

  NoRunbook:
    description: Manual failover without documented procedure
    rule: manual failover MUST have tested runbook

Navigation:
  Levels: @levels.sdd
  Patterns: @patterns.sdd
  Scaling: @scaling.sdd
  Data: @data.sdd
  Observability: @observability.sdd
  Networking: @networking.sdd
  Deployment: @deployment.sdd
  Project: @project/project.sdd
  ProjectArchitecture: @projects/vkard.az/architecture/INDEX.sdd
  Decisions: @projects/vkard.az/decisions/INDEX.sdd

State: +
  